9.339, 89.385, 89.432, 89.49, 89.525, 89.571, 89.618, 89.687, 89.745, 89.815, 89.885, 89.954, 90.012, 90.059, 90.14, 90.349, 90.953, 90.976, 91.022, 91.057, 91.127, 91.185, 91.243, 91.301, 91.359, 91.417, 91.498, 91.556, 91.638, 91.684, 91.742, 91.8, 91.882, 91.928, 92.044, 92.195, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.054, 93.147, 93.217, 93.286, 93.333, 93.379, 93.461, 93.577, 93.786, 93.855, 93.937, 93.995, 94.064, 94.122, 94.169, 94.215, 94.262, 94.32, 94.378, 94.401, 94.424, 94.459, 94.517, 94.575, 94.633, 94.691, 94.749, 94.796, 94.842, 94.958, 95.132, 96.247, 96.328, 96.653, 96.804, 96.874, 96.955, 97.013, 97.083, 97.141, 97.199, 97.234, 97.269, 97.327, 97.35, 97.373, 97.396, 97.489, 97.547, 97.663, 97.733, 97.849, 98.128], "text_sent": "Elizabeth B\u00e1thory was accused of burning young maids with hot irons. Of forcing others into freezing water. People said young women were dying in her household. The rumours reached the king. He sent Hungary's highest official, Gy\u00f6rgy Thurz\u00f3, to investigate. One December night, armed men arrived at her manor. B\u00e1thory was eating supper. Thurz\u00f3 wrote that his men had found one girl dead and another dying. B\u00e1thory was taken to the fortress above her home. Four servants, three women and a young man, were arrested as her alleged accomplices. Under torture, they confessed to dozens of killings. Three were executed within days. But B\u00e1thory was never tried. Nothing was ever proven against her in court. The case rested on witness testimony, and much of it was hearsay. The king pressed for a trial. Thurz\u00f3 prevented it. Thurz\u00f3 had fought the Ottomans beside her husband. Ferenc's dying wish was that his friend watch over her. Many historians think he confined her quietly rather than risk her beheading and the family's lands. She died in confinement. Then someone added the detail that would make her infamous. A century later, a priest wrote that she bathed in murdered girls' blood to keep her beauty. No witness against her had described that. No court ever heard her. But the legend had its monster: the Blood Countess. <break time=\"0.8s\"/> History remembers the victors. We remember the rest.", "src_md5": "9db405e1fe6a02f5ec12626dd541db41", "voice": "jfIS2w2yJi0grJZPyEsk", "model": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.5, "similarity_boost": 0.75, "style": 0.0, "use_speaker_boost": true}, "seed": 1611}